{"event":"trap-result","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-0","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-0/trap-0","ordinal":0,"result":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.14196","title":"Joint Quantization and Dynamic Partitioning for Context-Aware Edge Vision Transformers","authors":"Zhu Jiaqi , Jain Prakriti , Katare Dewant , Kumar Saurabh","submitted":"10 Nov 2026","abstract":"Deploying vision transformer models across connected and automated vehicles (CAVs) requires joint management of computation and Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) communication. Static split-inference methods use fixed partition points and cannot adapt to time-varying link bandwidth, processing load, and concurrent multi-stream traffic. This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes. The joint partitioning and mixed-precision allocation problem is formulated as a Mixed-Integer Linear Program (MILP) that minimizes compute and communication energy subject to end-to-end latency and task-quality constraints. The framework is evaluated on Jetson Orin Nano (8~GB), Jetson Orin NX (16~GB), and Jetson AGX Orin (32~GB) nodes using OPV2V-driven multi-stream workloads with $K \\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}$. Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by $33.3$--$36.0\\%$ relative to a Uniform INT8 split. At $K=8$, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by $34.0\\%$ on the Orin Nano and $33.1\\%$ on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to $3.1$--$4.1$ percentage points in $\\mathrm{mAP}_{50}$ relative to FP16 execution.","numbers":["Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by 33.3–36.0% relative to a Uniform INT8 split.","At K=8, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by 34.0% on the Orin Nano and 33.1% on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to 3.1–4.1 percentage points in mAP_50 relative to FP16 execution."],"method":["This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":6225,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14196","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.14196"}}
{"event":"branch-done","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-0","parentForkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","branch":0,"outcome":{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.14196","title":"Joint Quantization and Dynamic Partitioning for Context-Aware Edge Vision Transformers","authors":"Zhu Jiaqi , Jain Prakriti , Katare Dewant , Kumar Saurabh","submitted":"10 Nov 2026","abstract":"Deploying vision transformer models across connected and automated vehicles (CAVs) requires joint management of computation and Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) communication. Static split-inference methods use fixed partition points and cannot adapt to time-varying link bandwidth, processing load, and concurrent multi-stream traffic. This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes. The joint partitioning and mixed-precision allocation problem is formulated as a Mixed-Integer Linear Program (MILP) that minimizes compute and communication energy subject to end-to-end latency and task-quality constraints. The framework is evaluated on Jetson Orin Nano (8~GB), Jetson Orin NX (16~GB), and Jetson AGX Orin (32~GB) nodes using OPV2V-driven multi-stream workloads with $K \\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}$. Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by $33.3$--$36.0\\%$ relative to a Uniform INT8 split. At $K=8$, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by $34.0\\%$ on the Orin Nano and $33.1\\%$ on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to $3.1$--$4.1$ percentage points in $\\mathrm{mAP}_{50}$ relative to FP16 execution.","numbers":["Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by 33.3–36.0% relative to a Uniform INT8 split.","At K=8, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by 34.0% on the Orin Nano and 33.1% on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to 3.1–4.1 percentage points in mAP_50 relative to FP16 execution."],"method":["This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":6225,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14196","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.14196"}}}
